{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The protocol claims to prevent silent displacement via a durable intent record placed in a 'holder-local but replicated store,' yet it provides no causal mechanism ensuring the reclaimer can actually observe that record after the holder crashes. By omitting replication atomicity, consistency, or read-path guarantees, the target leaves a visibility gap: the intent may be durably persisted on the crashed node's local replica while remaining invisible to the reclaimer. After the epoch lease expires, the reclaimer observes no un-finalized intent, satisfies both reclamation conditions, and reclaims the workspace—silently displacing completed evidence that was flushed after the local intent write but before the crash. The appeal to 'exactly-once semantics enforced by epoch monotonicity' is vacuous because epoch counters on the holder provide no causal guarantee that intent records propagate to the reclaimer's observation point before reclamation proceeds.","counterexample":[{"event":"Holder writes intent to its local replica of the intent store."},{"event":"Holder durably flushes completed evidence to the workspace."},{"event":"Holder crashes before the intent replicates to the store nodes visible to the reclaimer."},{"event":"Reclaimer observes no un-finalized intent and an expired epoch lease."},{"event":"Reclaimer reclaims the workspace, silently displacing the completed evidence."}]}]}